WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.750
- What I would like to do

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.180
is I'm gonna talk for
about hopefully 30 minutes,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.740
'cause I do want this to
be more of a discussion,

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.713
and have question and answer.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.830
What I would like to do

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.790
is I will be speaking somewhat formally,

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.090
somewhat informally.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.960
I'll try to show some images

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.960
and then leave plenty of time at the end

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.970
for questions and answers.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.470
So, what I'd like to do in some ways

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.260
is to lay out my two main arguments

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.400
that I would like to
make in this talk today.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.730
And then I'm gonna provide
three case studies,

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.063
one in Guam, one in Malaysia,
and one in Hong Kong.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.370
First of all Erik, I really
appreciate your introduction,

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.850
and in many ways it makes me feel

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.810
like I succeeded on some level,

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:54.620
'cause my goal was to tell
the story of the refugee camps

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.690
and what is the actual
politics of the place

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.870
that hosted the refugee camps.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.450
As many of you know
there's a lot of literature

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.740
on Vietnamese American
studies, more broadly.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:10.860
In my work I chose not to focus
on the politics of Vietnam

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.570
or why people left,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.150
nor do I really look
at resettlement issues,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.670
whether it's the United
States, Australia, Canada,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:18.830
or in Europe.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:20.320
Instead, what I'm interested in

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.450
how did the politics of place matter,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.660
in these various places
that hosted the camps.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.940
I would argue that we learn a great deal

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.140
about the specificity

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.690
of who gets to be a
refugee and who doesn't,

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.570
based on the regional
dynamics of that place.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:38.890
It made a great deal of difference

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.230
if you were in the Philippines,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.960
versus if you were in Malaysia,

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.870
or if you were in Hong Kong.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.610
How did it matter, for example,

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.230
if we're thinking about Malaysia,

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.950
the politics of Mahathir, or
Malaysian racial politics,

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.890
versus politics of the Marcos era,

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.690
in the Philippines in the 1980s.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.800
One of my key arguments is that,

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.800
the dynamics of the local place mattered,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.320
the refugee status was just not
a matter of what the UN said

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.030
or what the United States said,

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.610
but mattered a lot about the
local politics of the places

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.053
that hosted the camps.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.660
That's argument one.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.590
I'll just say briefly,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.957
I'm not discussing the
Philippines in this talk,

00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:22.483
I'm very happy to talk about
the Philippines as well,

00:02:23.873 --> 00:02:25.450
and I'm really interested that.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.283
If I don't talk about the Philippines

00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:29.930
and your interest in the
Philippines, please ask questions.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:33.960
Secondly, I'm also interested
in Asian American studies

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.540
and in critical refugee studies.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.700
I would like this talk to
be of interest and value

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.260
to people who are just in
Southeast Asian history,

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.780
but also to scholars of
Asian American studies

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.730
and those intersections
with refugee studies.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.150
In my book, I hope that
I show multiplicity,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.000
agency, and complexity of
the Vietnamese men and women

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.003
I write about.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.150
Very simply, and this I
think should be obvious,

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.800
the individuals I write
about were far from passive,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.380
they were not object.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.000
They were in fact political actors.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.980
I think I managed to write the book

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.230
without ever saying, "the
plight of the refugee".

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.250
Rather I'm much more interested

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.760
in the decisions people were making,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.510
how they negotiated,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.540
how they understood their
time within the camps,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.880
whether or not they
wanted to then resettle

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.290
in the United States,

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.020
or another settlement country,

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.570
or even if they wanted
to go back to Vietnam.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:31.780
My story in many ways,

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.040
I think complicates an image
of Vietnamese refugees,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.320
which I think is still focused
much disproportionately

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.790
on the 1975 migration,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.210
and the individuals who
resettled after 1975,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.990
well often not recognizing

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:52.110
the hundreds of thousands
who left after 1975

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.300
in the late 70S, the 80S,
and even through the 1990s.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.830
By showing those variations

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.520
by thinking about Chinese Vietnamese,

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.560
by thinking about people who
left from Northern Vietnam,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.940
or Central Vietnam

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.830
in relationship to those
coming from Southern Vietnam,

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.740
I'm hoping to show a much
more complicated story,

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:14.770
which I also connect
with diasporic activism

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:18.230
among Vietnamese who resettled
both in the United States,

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:19.830
as well as in Australia.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.870
So I look at the intersections
of Vietnamese in the camps

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.220
and diasporic actors

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.650
disproportionately in the United States,

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.350
but to some extent in Australia as well.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.660
So those are the two main arguments.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:32.493
What I would like to do

00:04:32.493 --> 00:04:37.223
is begin by bringing us to
where I started this project,

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:40.903
which was with this image.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.120
I found this image when I
was doing archival research,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.730
actually about a different project

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.610
related to refugee camps
in the United States,

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.900
and this is an image of
Vietnamese in Guam, in 1975.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.140
It is a group of Vietnamese,

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.790
as probably most of you
in this Zoom Room know,

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.340
approximately 100,000 Vietnamese leave

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.183
at the end of the US war.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.930
The majority of these men and women

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.693
come to the United
States, Canada or France.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:12.480
What I did not know

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.200
was that most of them go to Guam first.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.180
In some ways, if you
think about it makes sense

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.100
that you don't just show
up from a boat in Saigon

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.560
to California,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.070
but the United States brought
most of these individuals

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.663
to Guam for screening.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.130
Most of these individuals did in fact

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.900
want to come to the United
States or a resettlement country.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.090
But some did not,

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.800
about 2000 did not.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.487
I found this image and I was just like,

00:05:37.487 --> 00:05:39.267
"What is going on?

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:43.317
"Who are these men who
want go back to Vietnam?"

00:05:43.317 --> 00:05:45.027
"How are they protesting with this image

00:05:45.027 --> 00:05:47.080
"of Ho Chi Minh in the background?"

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.070
'Cause what I had learned and
served as general common sense

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.570
is that the majority Vietnamese Americans

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.090
in the United States,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.300
have identified with anti-communism,

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:57.830
there debates over images

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.450
and serve the representations
of Ho Chi Minh,

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.250
so I was really struck by this image

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.130
and wanted to know what was going on.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.810
Initially I thought I'd find no documents

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:06.960
and that was totally wrong.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.560
There's tons of documents about this.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.110
This in many ways came
to my research questions.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.020
In this image

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.780
we had men who wanted
to go back to Vietnam

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.350
and did not wanna resettle
in the United States.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.750
Just quickly to locate where we are,

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.430
Guam is right here,

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.670
it's in the Pacific.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.640
It's actually not part of Southeast Asia,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.250
it's part of the Pacific islands,

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.280
it is quite far from Southeast Asia.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.250
But the United States brought Vietnamese

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.320
from Southern Vietnam to Guam for staging.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.320
This is largely because Guam
is a US colony or territory.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.330
It is 4,000 miles from Hawaii,

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:49.070
is almost 6,000 miles from California.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:51.160
It became the primary staging ground

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.880
for this 1975 generation of Vietnamese

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.730
who were trying to leave the country

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.800
in the fall of South Vietnam,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.600
and resettle in the United States.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.090
The United States is able
to bring 100,000 people

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:08.170
to Guam because they do
not have to ask permission.

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:11.200
Guam again, as basically a US colony,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.990
the United States has
multiple military bases

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.820
and the Guam government
simply could not say no.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.240
And so Guam agrees to
accept all of these people,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.030
and they were on these sort
of makeshift refugee camps

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:26.643
within Guam.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:29.470
What I learned

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:33.100
is that most people (indistinct)
go to the United States.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:38.100
But about 2000 choose instead
that they made a mistake.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.740
That they did not in fact want to move on.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:41.870
So here's an image from...

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.070
These are all from the
"Pacific Daily News,"

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:46.430
which is the Guam newspaper in English.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:47.940
That you should know that
these do not represent

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.220
a tomorrow perspective.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:53.403
This is really sort of an
American focus newspaper.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.040
But why did people want to go back?

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:02.483
Who were these 2000 who
wanted to return to Vietnam?

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.850
I would suggest to you
that most of them...

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:09.683
I'm gonna stop sharing

00:08:09.683 --> 00:08:11.470
so I can see a few of those faces.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:14.270
Most of them were young men.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:17.500
The assumption is that they'd
all want come to America,

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.490
but many of these were young men,

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.330
about 80% of them were young men

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:24.740
who had been in the South Vietnamese Navy.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.090
But had been only 18, 19 years old,

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.310
and many of them were
like on a ship at sea.

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:31.450
They were in the Navy.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:35.247
So one day Saigon falls and
the captain of the ship says,

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:36.927
"We're not going back to port.

00:08:36.927 --> 00:08:38.800
"We're all gonna go to Guam."

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.400
And so these young men
found themselves on Guam

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.280
and were confused.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:45.330
They wanted to go home.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.500
They had no interest in going America,

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.810
at least not without their families.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.330
Others were older,

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.090
some had been separated
from their families,

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.550
and so we're unable to sort of...

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.790
didn't wanna continue on their own.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:01.530
Others were elderly people
who decided that in fact,

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.923
they didn't wanna take their
chances in the United States.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:07.660
Some of their stories are very striking,

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.350
and I've written about
this in some detail.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:12.080
The most traumatic is a group of officers

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.090
who flew airplanes out
of Saigon to Thailand

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.500
and could not go back to Vietnam,

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.300
unbeknownst to them.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.320
The US actually, there's
good documentation

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.630
that they were drugged,
and put onto airplanes

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.270
and brought to Guam.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.770
These men felt as if they were kidnapped.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.993
That they did not mean to flee.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:33.500
And so on Guam these...

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.100
And it is disproportionately men.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.130
Most of the people protesting were men,

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:40.120
there were some women
but much smaller numbers.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.100
They wanna go back to Vietnam,

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.700
and so they begin protesting.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:47.570
These protests begin to escalate.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:48.403
The United States

00:09:48.403 --> 00:09:49.750
honestly doesn't know
what to do with them.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.260
They don't wanna force them
to come to the United States,

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.120
they also don't really
know how to bring them back

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.330
to Vietnam,

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:57.990
and the Guamanian
government wants them off.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:09:59.607
The Guamanian government is like,

00:09:59.607 --> 00:10:01.377
"We don't need this headache.

00:10:01.377 --> 00:10:02.397
"Who are these people?

00:10:02.397 --> 00:10:03.817
"You said this was going to be brief,

00:10:03.817 --> 00:10:05.817
"and now people are burning down buildings

00:10:05.817 --> 00:10:07.850
"and marching up and down."

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.360
I just wanna show you
some of these images.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.810
I was not actually able to
include these in the book,

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:14.010
'cause they're from the
"Pacific Daily News"

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:16.580
their rights were too complicated.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.620
But the images are remarkable.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.660
Here's one of the individuals
who left the airplane,

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.370
he did not wanna come.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.130
You begin to see images
of people protesting

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:28.170
with English language signs,

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.810
you can see they're familiar with English.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.740
Many of them had worked
with the US Military.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:35.820
You get images like this one,

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.823
where the repatriates
walk out and protest,

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.403
hunger strikes.

00:10:42.497 --> 00:10:44.727
"Hunger strike until die".

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:49.623
They burned down buildings on the bases.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:54.710
Eventually the US government gives in,

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:56.200
for lack of a better word,

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:58.617
and says, "Fine, you
can go back to Vietnam,

00:10:58.617 --> 00:11:02.560
"and we're gonna give you
your own ship to return on."

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:04.270
They give them this commercial vessel,

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.110
the Thuong Tin (indistinct),

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:10.010
and they tell these young
men, the repatriates,

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.250
that they can return.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:12.770
Here's an image that really stood with me,

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.260
of them sort of celebrating this return.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.130
In this image I think you
can really see the youth

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:18.230
of these men.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.150
They were really quite young.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:23.370
You can see, I think in this
image their sense of joy

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.400
that they are going home.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.053
The goal is that they
will return to Vietnam.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:30.290
They do return,

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:34.120
this is on October of 1975.

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:36.988
Here again is an image of the ship.

00:11:36.988 --> 00:11:40.150
I had the great honor of
working with Tran Dinh Tru,

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.050
and my colleague Bac Hoai Tran,

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:44.350
to translate a Vietnamese memoir

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:47.083
about the ship that returned to Vietnam.

00:11:48.917 --> 00:11:51.530
In some ways quite tragically,

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.720
and I think this might
surprise anyone in this room.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:57.440
The Vietnamese government
was not sympathetic

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.260
towards this group of repatriates.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:03.190
They saw them as potential
CIA agents, as suspect,

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.400
as potential infiltrators,

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:07.030
did not take them at their word,

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:10.120
and they were brought
to re-education camps,

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:13.300
when they returned in 1975.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:14.133
In particular,

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:15.970
the author I worked with Tran Dinh Tru,

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.923
was in prison for 12 years.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.953
Very traumatic experiences.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.020
But why do I find the story so compelling,

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.440
and how did it bring me on
this journey through the book?

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:28.420
First of all,

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.590
I think it's important that
we begin the story in Guam.

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.590
Do you think Guam's politics matter?

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:35.440
Matters that it was a colony.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.820
Matters that Guam could not
say no to the United States.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:42.610
It mattered that Guam had this
network of US Military bases

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.680
because of the history
of US militarism on Guam.

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:48.030
It's also important to recognize

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.350
that the Guamanian politicians
did try to do their best

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:53.000
to manage the situation.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.100
They felt responsible to
both Guamanians, Chamorros,

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.890
as well as American Military personnel.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:01.870
They were trying to come
up with a way to navigate

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:04.970
both the pressures they had on
them from the US government,

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:06.550
and the pressures that they had

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.250
from these Vietnamese repatriates.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:13.050
Secondly, I think it shows a
very dynamic story of activism

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.170
in one and an unexpected place.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:19.200
Here we see a group of
Vietnamese who are mobilized,

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.020
who recognize their
ability to create pressure

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:25.180
on the US government, and
on the Guamanian government,

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.810
and seek to in fact,
direct to their own goals,

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:31.420
which in their case was not
coming to the United States,

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.323
but rather was returning to Vietnam.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:37.060
Thirdly, I think it's a
really complicated story

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:39.740
'cause there's a part of me
that wants to in many ways

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:42.710
celebrate their activism,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:46.060
celebrate their sort of political acumen,

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:49.860
and yet their stories, one which
is tragic in multiple ways.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.280
Because when they return,

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.570
they are imprisoned, and
they're seen as suspect.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:57.610
Many then do come to the United States

00:13:57.610 --> 00:13:59.360
in the late '70s and early '80s,

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.860
or through the ODP, or
because they leave later.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:06.210
But it is not a story
where they triumphed.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:09.240
In many ways it is a much
more ambivalent story,

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.210
and one that I think demonstrates

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.763
that they did not know
what was gonna happen.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.290
I think in many ways,

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:18.120
I think of Tran Dinh Tru,

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:20.960
he talked about leaving
Northern Vietnam in 1954

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:23.060
and never seeing his family
again in North Vietnam.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:25.230
That was his experience
when he moved South.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.100
When he thought, when he
was separated from his wife,

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:28.510
that he would never see her again.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:32.090
That was a real feeling,
and it was legitimate.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.750
And that he did not
realize when he returned,

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.290
quite how long he would be in prison,

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.170
or the trauma that that
would inflict upon him

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.400
or his family.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:42.095
He does come to the
United States in the '90s

00:14:42.095 --> 00:14:44.770
and I had the good fortune of meeting him.

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:46.520
That is where the story begins.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:47.900
The key points are one,

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:49.700
the politics of Guam,

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.040
and second, the questions of activism

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.563
and third, the complexities.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:55.453
So with that,

00:14:55.453 --> 00:14:57.130
I wanna turn to my next case,

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.103
which is that of Malaysia.

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:03.393
Malaysia I'm gonna show you the map again,

00:15:04.681 --> 00:15:05.514
here we go.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.583
If we come here and we go to Malaysia,

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:14.000
and I also see my
colleague Quan Tran here,

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.390
I know you've done a great
deal of really fantastic work

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:17.223
on Malaysia.

00:15:17.223 --> 00:15:18.710
I hope we can talk afterwards as well.

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:20.180
But for those of you less familiar,

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:21.520
maybe with the geography,

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:22.450
here we have Malaysia.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.400
Malaysia is two parts,
Peninsular Malaysia,

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.490
and Eastern Malaysia.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:29.359
We have Sabah here it's
partially important

00:15:29.359 --> 00:15:31.550
I'm gonna start with the Filipino
refugees coming into Sabah

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.163
as well at the same time.

00:15:34.003 --> 00:15:35.560
So we have a large number of Vietnamese.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.260
This is the era which in
the West from Americans,

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:40.980
and in English language newspapers

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.739
often will start referring
to the "Boat people."

00:15:43.739 --> 00:15:46.340
This gonna be a large numbers
of Vietnamese leaving.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.920
This is after some of
the re-education camps,

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.190
really some people in the late '70s,

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:54.190
you also have the Vietnamese government,

00:15:55.204 --> 00:15:56.140
having more collectivization,

00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:58.310
sending people to new economic zones.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:01.063
This leads people to leave Vietnam.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:04.920
As you're gonna here,
see yourself, Vietnam,

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.740
people either went to
Thailand or to Malaysia.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:11.203
You can see here the geography
was the closest proximity.

00:16:13.092 --> 00:16:14.080
At this moment,

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.570
the largest group in Malaysia
people go to this Island

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:20.990
in Pulau Bidong which is in Terengganu,

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:25.360
which is a couple of hours
away from Kuala Lumpur.

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:27.680
In some ways in a sort of regional,

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.653
the Northeastern region of Malaysia.

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:32.660
What I would say in this case,

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:35.490
which I find quite interesting

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:38.030
is again, thinking about
the Malaysian politics

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:41.250
and how this fits into our story.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.220
So in the United States,

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:49.220
US politicians saw all
Vietnamese as anti-communists,

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:50.440
as "deserving."

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.530
There's this clearly
anti-refugee sentiment

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:53.363
in the United States,

00:16:53.363 --> 00:16:55.210
as well as anti-Asian politics,

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:59.570
but rhetorically they're often
framed as anti-communists,

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:03.560
and in some ways deserving of US aid.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.048
But this was not the way
it was seen in Malaysia.

00:17:06.048 --> 00:17:08.780
I think that Malaysia plays
a really complicated role.

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:11.660
On the one hand it offers
refuge to thousands,

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:13.890
tens of thousands of Vietnamese,

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:15.510
and yet on the other hand,

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:18.500
they begin casting people off to sea,

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.040
pushing boats away, off to Indonesia,

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:24.340
which results in thousands
of people drowning,

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:26.560
and ends up having a very harsh policy

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:28.850
towards these Vietnamese boats.

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:31.700
And so there's a real
conflicted relationship.

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:33.080
On this island, Pulau Bidong,

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:36.290
the conditions are quite
difficult and complicated.

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:39.320
I had the good fortune of being
able to go to Pulau Bidong

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:40.540
about a year ago.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:42.650
This is today.

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:45.823
These are the archival
images from the 1970s.

00:17:47.562 --> 00:17:48.580
(speaker mumbling)

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:49.413
Oh yes.

00:17:49.413 --> 00:17:50.484
Thank you.

00:17:50.484 --> 00:17:51.410
Thank you for putting up Quan's article.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:52.353
It's fantastic.

00:17:54.381 --> 00:17:55.973
And here we have,

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.223
again, images from Pulau Bidong.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:01.900
But one of the things
that I want to talk about

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:05.890
is how did Malaysia see
these Vietnamese differently

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:07.393
than the United States?

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:09.460
One thing that is notable

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:13.500
is that a disproportionate
number of these Vietnamese

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.873
were Chinese Vietnamese.

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:19.900
This is notable because in Malaysia

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:23.170
this meant that they were seen differently

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:26.220
than they were either in Hong
Kong or in the United States.

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:29.890
This is because Malaysia has a population,

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:32.740
which is about 40% Malay,

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:34.880
about 20 to 30% Chinese ethnicity,

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.550
and about 10 to 50% South Asian.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:40.390
What this means is that

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:43.270
there is a way in which in Malaysia,

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:48.260
these incoming Vietnamese
were seen as "Chinese."

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:50.350
It meant that in Malaysia,

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:51.600
there was anxiety

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.650
about them actually
bringing communism with them

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:56.210
to Malaysia.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.600
From the United States point of view,

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:59.120
this would seem counter-intuitive.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:02.000
These are individuals who are
fleeing a communist country.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:02.833
These are individuals

00:19:02.833 --> 00:19:05.380
who generally narrate their experiences

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:07.240
as being anti-communist.

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:10.450
Yet the Malay newspapers, the Malay texts,

00:19:10.450 --> 00:19:15.450
often their xenophobia took
a lens of anti-Chineseness,

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:18.540
and also anti-communism.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:21.000
So if we think today about
the ways in the United States,

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:22.260
for example,

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.340
anti-Syrian refugee politics

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:26.910
can often come into this idea

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:29.330
of seeing Syrians as terrorists,

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:32.830
even though the Syrians
are fleeing terrorist acts,

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:34.700
here we see something similar

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:37.870
where we see Chinese
Vietnamese somehow being blamed

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:40.300
or associated with the very government

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:41.933
that they are trying to leave.

00:19:42.831 --> 00:19:44.990
I just wanna give you a few examples.

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:47.070
So for example, in Mastika,

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.030
which was a Malay language newspaper

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:51.533
that was very popular
in Malaysia at the time,

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:55.910
that they would argue that
rather than seeing them

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:57.910
Malaysia's anti-communist haven,

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:00.390
it said that they suspected
that the communist agents

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:01.850
were gonna go and make contacts

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.560
with the Malaysian Communist Party.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:07.000
The New Strait Times explicitly said

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.890
that this was essentially their fear.

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.810
The Far Eastern Economic Review

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:13.960
reported that the government feared Hanoi

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:16.860
was intentionally "planting agents"

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:19.240
among the so-called refugees.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.840
There was a letter to the editor

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:24.167
in the Far Eastern
Economic Review that said,

00:20:24.167 --> 00:20:27.327
"The communist underground
in Peninsular Malaysia

00:20:27.327 --> 00:20:32.327
"led by Chin Peng was and still
is dominated by the Chinese.

00:20:32.437 --> 00:20:34.317
"The idea that Chinese are communist

00:20:34.317 --> 00:20:36.977
"is embodied in the minds of the Malays.

00:20:36.977 --> 00:20:38.367
"They form an equation.

00:20:38.367 --> 00:20:40.137
"Chinese equals communists,

00:20:40.137 --> 00:20:41.917
"Chinese equals refugees.

00:20:41.917 --> 00:20:44.940
"Therefore refugees equals communists."

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:47.440
And the government argued
for constant vigilance

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:49.457
and it even suggested that many of those

00:20:49.457 --> 00:20:52.677
"who were running away' had
supported the Viet Cong."

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.349
So there was this, I would
say, real ambilevous.

00:20:56.349 --> 00:20:59.570
On the one hand, I actually
want to recognize that Malaysia

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:02.400
took in tens of thousands of people.

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:05.640
And then a moment when we see
countries around the globe,

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:07.820
including in terms of its
particularly the United States,

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:09.610
refusing refugees,

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:13.080
Malaysia actually did
face a fair deal of burden

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:15.320
taking in large numbers of people.

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:17.040
On the other hand,

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.820
there was a great deal of xenophobia,

00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:21.480
which I think we see in
almost any nation state.

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:22.330
In addition,

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:25.520
this sort of rhetoric of
fear about the Vietnamese

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.660
as infiltrating Malaysia,

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:30.270
that's why they had to be kept
on Pulau Bidong, an island,

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:33.453
we don't wanna let them
associate with Malaysians.

00:21:34.926 --> 00:21:36.300
This will lead to in many ways,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.680
Malaysia's much harsher policy
where they begin instituting,

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:42.421
which is pushing these boats off to sea.

00:21:42.421 --> 00:21:45.610
This results in people both dying,

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.470
and great deals of trauma

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:50.460
and will lead to Mahathir's comments.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:53.010
Prime Minister, Mohamad Mahathir

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.830
becomes one of the most
important Malaysian politician

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:59.480
of the 20th century and into the 21st.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:02.780
He is still the Deputy Prime
Minister at this point.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:05.117
And he says in 1979,

00:22:05.117 --> 00:22:07.597
"Our patience is fully extended.

00:22:07.597 --> 00:22:10.947
"If 500,000 immigrants
try to land in Britain,

00:22:10.947 --> 00:22:13.017
"they might probably shoot them.

00:22:13.017 --> 00:22:14.957
"We've already been accused by Newsweek

00:22:14.957 --> 00:22:16.837
"of shooting any illegal immigrants.

00:22:16.837 --> 00:22:20.397
"So we might as well do it
and justify their accusations.

00:22:20.397 --> 00:22:22.647
"We are tired of being nice and humane

00:22:22.647 --> 00:22:24.097
"and what did we get, nothing.

00:22:24.097 --> 00:22:25.223
"So why bother?

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:28.650
Mohathir's frustration broke all polite

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:30.700
or diplomatic pretenses,

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:32.160
and the "New York Times" reported

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:36.110
that shoot on site legislation
could be forthcoming.

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:37.500
His strong word succeeded

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:40.040
in capturing international intention,

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:41.950
gaining sort of momentum,

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:45.080
and forcing along with Hong
Kong and Great Britain,

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:48.760
the UNHCR to come up with a new formula

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:52.113
that would grant Vietnamese
rights to first asylum.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:54.210
I think this is interesting

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:56.280
because Malaysia as an
independent country,

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:58.180
which had been a colony,

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:01.690
is trying to both assert
its national sovereignty,

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:02.780
is trying to in some ways

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:06.650
throw the language of
humanitarianism back at the West,

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:08.640
and is trying to negotiate

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:13.080
essentially how to maintain
control over its borders.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.310
Even as the boarder is providing support

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:16.500
and the Red Crescent Society

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:17.750
provides a great deal of support

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:20.040
for the Vietnamese in the camps,

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:22.290
as well as in many ways

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:24.630
going alongside the Malay chauvinism,

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:28.203
that is very much apparent
in the Malaysian politics.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:34.030
This will result in the 1979
UNHCR Conference in Geneva.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:36.160
And this will create a framework

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.510
which will provide refugee status

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:41.130
and first asylum status for Vietnamese

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:45.520
who land in first asylum
countries from 1979 to 1989.

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:47.820
So essentially it's a
three part agreement.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:52.513
First, Vietnam will start
preventing Vietnamese

00:23:52.513 --> 00:23:54.430
from leaving so easily.

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:55.510
And it will also start

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:57.820
what's called The Orderly
Departure Program,

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:01.100
which will allow Vietnamese
to leave from Vietnam.

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:02.696
That program doesn't really get going

00:24:02.696 --> 00:24:03.529
through the late '80s,

00:24:03.529 --> 00:24:07.330
but it gets started in 1980, '79-1980.

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:08.180
and it's actually,

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:09.770
I think one of the
missing things in my book.

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:12.130
I think there's a great book
to be written about the ODP

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:15.160
and hopefully someone in this
group might be doing that.

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:18.260
Secondly, it said the
first asylum countries

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.240
will accept Vietnamese.

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:22.400
They will not push them off.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.250
This doesn't mean it totally stops,

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:25.830
it does keep happening,

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:28.803
but at least in principle
there will be first asylum.

00:24:29.852 --> 00:24:32.520
Secondly, it says that the United States,

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:35.410
Europe, resettlement countries,

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:37.790
will accept everyone who
shows up in the camps.

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:40.140
They will all get resettled
in a third country.

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:42.853
That's the 1979 agreement.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:45.020
Finally, at this point,

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:47.650
the last thing I wanna add
before turning to Hong Kong,

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:50.543
is we begin to get Vietnamese activism.

00:24:52.490 --> 00:24:53.530
Oh goodness, here we go.

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:55.210
We began to get Vietnamese activism.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:56.170
I liked this image here.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:58.420
This is a group of Vietnamese Americans

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:03.130
advocating for support
of this 1979 Agreement.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.960
And you began to have
organizations like IRAC,

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:09.080
which was the Inner China
Research Action Committee

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:10.860
lobbying the UN.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:13.297
You begin to have groups
like Boat People SOS,

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:17.170
becoming active in refugee politics,

00:25:17.170 --> 00:25:18.480
and recognizing

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:21.070
that if they wanted to be
able to protect Vietnamese

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:22.720
who want to leave the country,

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:23.810
that they would be able...

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:26.890
they needed to start
sort of finding power.

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.860
That was both through the United Nations

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:31.240
and through the United States.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:34.250
These groups, both IRAC,
and Boat People SOS

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:38.600
began lobbying both the
UN and US politicians.

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:41.880
We see this emerging in the late 1970s.

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:43.410
This is just an image from, again,

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:44.340
my most recent trip,

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:47.270
and again, Quan has written
much more about this.

00:25:47.270 --> 00:25:50.880
I found this a really moving
memorial on Pulau Bidong.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:52.880
Someone has been reciting

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:56.790
inside of a solar powered
musical apparatus.

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:58.580
When you go through this memorial,

00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:00.560
you actually hear music

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:03.403
even though it is generally unpopulated.

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:07.933
Last point before we turn
to questions, Hong Kong.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:10.890
Key points about Malaysia.

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:12.570
Malaysian sovereignty,

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:15.120
specificities of Malaysian politics,

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.113
beginnings of diasporic activism.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:19.620
Hong Kong.

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:22.340
So this is a map, I think Carina,

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:23.650
this might even be one of the maps

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:25.220
that you and I both use.

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:28.130
This is one of the maps
of the various camps

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:29.483
within Hong Kong.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:32.780
I'm gonna actually,

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:34.680
this is from the artwork from UC Irvine,

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:35.513
and this is also

00:26:35.513 --> 00:26:38.860
from the University of
California Irvine, archive.

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.520
I'll show you the images at the end.

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:42.400
So why Hong Kong?

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:45.903
Hong Kong becomes a key
site in the late 1980s.

00:26:46.775 --> 00:26:50.450
I think that we see here why
the politics of place matter.

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:52.143
Hong Kong is a British colony.

00:26:53.384 --> 00:26:54.990
It was North of Vietnam.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:56.570
So you've got a larger number of people

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:59.130
from Northern Vietnam coming to Hong Kong.

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:01.180
This will make a big
difference to how refugees

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:03.420
or the Vietnamese are seen in Hong Kong,

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.230
because they are coming
from Northern Vietnam.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:07.703
But Hong Kong is British.

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:09.730
British colonialism,

00:27:09.730 --> 00:27:11.600
they're supposed to be humanitarian.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:13.497
There's a lot of pressure on Hong Kong,

00:27:13.497 --> 00:27:16.390
and they are very proud that
they do not push boats off

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:18.280
the way the Malaysians did,

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:20.950
but they do put the Vietnamese in camps.

00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:24.440
But from 1979 to 1988,

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:27.430
Hong Kong accepts all
of the Vietnamese coming

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:30.630
and all the Vietnamese
who show up in Hong Kong,

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:31.810
get refugee status.

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:32.930
And again, a lot of this,

00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:34.643
and Carina has done work on this as well,

00:27:34.643 --> 00:27:37.040
and you might wanna add in later Carina

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:38.140
when we have a conversation.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:41.173
We have large numbers coming to Hong Kong.

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:44.480
What happens though is
that Hong Kong's politics

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:46.150
are against specific.

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:48.340
Two things more than two,
but two I wanna mention

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:50.060
make Hong Kong really different.

00:27:50.060 --> 00:27:51.860
One, if you are Chinese

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:55.170
and you wanna come to Hong Kong in 1985,

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:56.060
you're deported.

00:27:56.060 --> 00:27:57.260
You're sent back.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:59.040
So if you are a Hong Kong Chinese person

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.510
and your cousin comes over from Shanghai,

00:28:01.510 --> 00:28:04.960
unllegally undocumented,
you got sent back.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:06.717
That meant a lot of people
in Hong Kong were like,

00:28:06.717 --> 00:28:08.017
"Wait a second,

00:28:08.017 --> 00:28:10.187
"why do the Vietnamese get to stay?

00:28:10.187 --> 00:28:11.687
"Why did they get resettled?

00:28:11.687 --> 00:28:16.107
"If my spouse from China
doesn't get to, why not?

00:28:16.107 --> 00:28:19.230
"Why did these Vietnamese
get this special treatment?"

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:24.230
Secondly, Hong Kong is going
to be come China in 1997.

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:27.300
There's this pressure on Hong Kong,

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:29.820
to navigate this transition

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:32.780
from the British to Chinese sovereignty.

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:37.140
Hong Kong doesn't have a
lot of independent power.

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:40.750
What I argue is that
by changing the policy,

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:42.440
vis-a-vis the Vietnamese,

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:44.710
it is a way for Hong Kong politicians

00:28:44.710 --> 00:28:47.080
to reassert their own agenda,

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:49.870
at a moment where they
don't have a lot of control.

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:51.730
And so that really shapes

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:54.500
what will become the
fate of these Vietnamese

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:56.730
in the late 1980s.

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:57.793
So what happens?

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:00.040
A lot happens.

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:05.040
In 1988, Hong Kong basically
rips up the UNHCR Accord.

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:08.570
They say, "Too many people are coming,"

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:09.560
and really importantly,

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:11.077
and this brings in the United States,

00:29:11.077 --> 00:29:13.790
"United States is not
resettling these people."

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:16.697
United States says, "These
people are from North Vietnam,

00:29:16.697 --> 00:29:18.327
"they're not South Vietnamese,

00:29:18.327 --> 00:29:20.247
"they're not really refugees,

00:29:20.247 --> 00:29:23.260
"and so they're not our problem."

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:24.380
The British don't take them.

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:26.247
The British say, "We've
taken 10,000 people,

00:29:26.247 --> 00:29:27.640
"not our problem."

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:29.317
And so Hong Kong is saying,

00:29:29.317 --> 00:29:31.430
"Well, what are we going to do then?"

00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:33.590
And so you should know
that the United States

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:36.300
in many ways passes the buck,

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:39.420
in a way and avoids responsibility.

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:40.350
And so Hong Kong,

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:42.497
the politicians say, "Then fine.

00:29:42.497 --> 00:29:43.637
"Then we're gonna change.

00:29:43.637 --> 00:29:44.927
"We can't take this anymore.

00:29:44.927 --> 00:29:46.977
"We need to change this policy

00:29:46.977 --> 00:29:49.507
"otherwise people are gonna
be in camps for years,

00:29:49.507 --> 00:29:52.187
"and we're gonna have China
here in the next 10 years.

00:29:52.187 --> 00:29:53.727
"We need to change.

00:29:53.727 --> 00:29:55.317
"And the change is this.

00:29:55.317 --> 00:29:57.807
"You'll no longer
automatically be a refugee.

00:29:57.807 --> 00:30:00.297
"Instead, you will become an asylum seeker

00:30:00.297 --> 00:30:02.287
"and we will interview you.

00:30:02.287 --> 00:30:06.517
"And you can prove to us that
you really are a refugee,

00:30:06.517 --> 00:30:09.687
"then yes, you can be
resettled in the United States

00:30:09.687 --> 00:30:11.527
"or Canada or Australia.

00:30:11.527 --> 00:30:13.677
"But if you cannot prove that,

00:30:13.677 --> 00:30:15.717
"if we think you are simply poor

00:30:15.717 --> 00:30:18.127
"or we think your story's too confusing,

00:30:18.127 --> 00:30:20.857
"or we think that you weren't prosecuted

00:30:20.857 --> 00:30:22.547
"or persecuted enough,

00:30:22.547 --> 00:30:24.497
"then you will be repatriated

00:30:24.497 --> 00:30:26.857
"and we will send you back to Vietnam."

00:30:27.700 --> 00:30:31.040
At first, the United Nations
is really opposed to this.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:32.487
They say, "No, that's wrong.

00:30:32.487 --> 00:30:33.417
"We can't be doing that.

00:30:33.417 --> 00:30:35.660
"We can't be sending people back."

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:37.320
But under Sergio Vieira,

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:38.690
which is actually really interesting,

00:30:38.690 --> 00:30:40.480
Sergio Vieira de Mello.

00:30:40.480 --> 00:30:42.480
He becomes an important
person in the United Nations.

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:44.663
He actually dies tragically in Iraq.

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:46.813
They say,

00:30:46.813 --> 00:30:48.820
"Actually, we think this is a good idea."

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.147
The UN turns around and says,

00:30:51.147 --> 00:30:54.340
"We are going to embrace this new model."

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:55.670
They come up with dates.

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:57.380
They're different in Hong Kong

00:30:57.380 --> 00:30:59.920
than they are in the other
Southeast Asian countries.

00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:01.760
But after these cutoff dates,

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:03.630
you will no longer be a refugee.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:05.850
You will have to make your case,

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:08.400
and if you can't, you will
be sent back to Vietnam.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:11.600
This leads to massive,

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:15.420
both legal and political
protests in the camps

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:16.563
in Hong Kong.

00:31:16.563 --> 00:31:18.490
This also happens in Malaysia as well,

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:20.603
but I focus my research on Hong Kong.

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:24.740
There's a way in which I argue here,

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:27.690
that there is a clash between

00:31:27.690 --> 00:31:29.310
where the Hong Kong government

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:31.790
liked to see as its humanitarian policies

00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:34.980
and credentials of safe harbor,

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:37.180
and what Vietnamese American activists

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:40.820
argued was a violation
of their human rights.

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:44.593
You can see here really a
contrast between these two.

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:48.730
This will lead to a great deal of activism

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:50.490
within the camps,

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.640
I'm gonna share my screen here again.

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:55.470
This will lead to both political activism.

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:58.130
You'll get lots of protests like this one,

00:31:58.130 --> 00:31:58.990
and for me also,

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:01.810
this image versus the one
in Guam are really striking.

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:04.490
Sort of similar protests,
but for different ends.

00:32:04.490 --> 00:32:07.900
These Vietnamese are protesting
both against the screening,

00:32:07.900 --> 00:32:10.518
many thought the screening
itself was wrong.

00:32:10.518 --> 00:32:13.010
They believed themselves to be refugees.

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:15.310
And secondly, against repatriation.

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:18.040
They do not wanna be sent back to Vietnam.

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:19.330
These are images largely

00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:22.050
from the English language
Hong Kong newspapers.

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:25.110
We are victims of the Hong Kong screening.

00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:26.230
Here again,

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:30.070
we see the Hong Kong security
forces entering these camps.

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:33.100
This leads to very violent
showdowns within the camps.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:34.350
This is in the late '90s

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:37.170
in the Whitehead detention centres.

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:39.240
These will become massive demonstrations

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:41.950
where again Vietnamese will protest,

00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:44.773
often very physically with their bodies,

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:46.930
through hunger strikes,

00:32:46.930 --> 00:32:49.493
through self-mutilation, through attacks,

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.820
arguing that they themselves are refugees

00:32:52.820 --> 00:32:54.830
and do not wanna go back.

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:56.840
Artistic representation,

00:32:56.840 --> 00:33:00.303
here are forced deportations
or repatriations.

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:04.640
In addition, these are
really traumatic images,

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:06.810
and there was even more of this.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:08.260
This archive is vast.

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:09.700
Again, Carina's done a great job,

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:11.240
both of documenting those stories

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:13.693
and of showing both images and video.

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:16.000
In addition, alongside this,

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:17.620
we get a lot of activism

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:19.550
from dye-spot Vietnamese Americans

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:21.140
and Vietnamese Australians.

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:23.490
So you begin to have groups, for example,

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:25.070
UC Irvine is a large centre

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:27.510
of Vietnamese American community,

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:29.620
project NOC, as well as other groups

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:31.580
begin to send volunteers

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:34.000
to work with Vietnamese in Hong Kong.

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:37.150
They provide English
classes and art classes.

00:33:37.150 --> 00:33:38.920
You also get lawyers.

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:41.270
So you begin to have
Americans send lawyers

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:44.000
to Hong Kong to help
advocate for the Vietnamese

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:45.240
within the camps.

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:47.330
They are fairly successful,

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:48.650
but not in a grand way.

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:49.483
Right?

00:33:49.483 --> 00:33:50.316
So they have individual cases,

00:33:50.316 --> 00:33:52.550
but they don't get the policy changed.

00:33:52.550 --> 00:33:54.960
You also get groups like Boat People SOS

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:56.240
that's transformed from groups

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:58.240
that actually saved people at sea

00:33:58.240 --> 00:34:01.010
to a really strong lobbying organization

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:02.350
in Northern Virginia,

00:34:02.350 --> 00:34:04.640
that is able to get bipartisan support,

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:05.950
but really a lot of support

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:09.240
from the Republican Congress members

00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:11.800
to try to again, prevent repatriations

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:14.150
or to provide more avenues for Vietnamese

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.640
to be resettled in the United States.

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:18.770
I'm interested in the dye-spot activism

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:21.660
and the networks between
the activism in the camps,

00:34:21.660 --> 00:34:25.163
as well as within the United
States and in Australia.

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:27.890
And so what happens in the end?

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:30.790
In the end, about 60,000 people

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:32.150
from Hong Kong are deported,

00:34:32.150 --> 00:34:33.670
about 30,000 more people

00:34:33.670 --> 00:34:35.690
from other camps throughout Southeast Asia

00:34:35.690 --> 00:34:37.480
are also repatriated.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:39.230
The numbers are really large.

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:42.343
Almost 100,000 people are
in the end repatriated.

00:34:43.318 --> 00:34:45.050
I think the story's
really important though,

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:45.970
for the same reasons.

00:34:45.970 --> 00:34:47.570
One place matters.

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:50.650
It mattered that you had
all Chinese majority.

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:54.330
It mattered that Hong
Kong was a British colony.

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.200
It matters that 1997,

00:34:56.200 --> 00:35:00.060
it was looming for these
Hong Kong politicians.

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:01.820
The part of Hong Kong are radical...

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:04.190
they're not worried about the
Vietnamese being communists,

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:05.680
like the Malaysians were.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:07.760
Rather, they have their
own set of concerns

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:10.480
and that shaped their policies.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:12.580
Secondly, they're against the activism.

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:15.280
The Vietnamese were extremely organized,

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.540
extremely strategic

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:21.830
in how they were creating both
legal and political campaigns

00:35:21.830 --> 00:35:23.700
to prevent repatriation

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:25.990
and also creating strategic alliances

00:35:25.990 --> 00:35:29.041
with Vietnamese already in the West.

00:35:29.041 --> 00:35:29.874
Okay.

00:35:29.874 --> 00:35:33.190
So those are in some ways the main points.

00:35:33.190 --> 00:35:34.180
Where to end?

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:37.300
I just wanna add that
these are obviously stories

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:38.950
that still matter today.

00:35:38.950 --> 00:35:41.520
I just wanna end with a few last images.

00:35:41.520 --> 00:35:43.630
There are stories just from two years ago,

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:47.330
many in this Zoom Room are
probably familiar with these.

00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:50.930
But the Trump administration
began deporting Vietnamese

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:53.500
who came in the 80s and 90s.

00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:56.610
Many of them came and
(indistinct) with the law,

00:35:56.610 --> 00:35:59.020
we're not able to become US citizens

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:01.330
and have been proactively deporting

00:36:01.330 --> 00:36:03.010
very vulnerable population

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:04.440
that had often been in the United States

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:06.890
for 10, 15, 20 years.

00:36:06.890 --> 00:36:08.180
This has again led

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:11.730
to Vietnamese diasporic activism.

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:14.110
This was from about two years ago.

00:36:14.110 --> 00:36:15.940
Here my politics, I like this one,

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:17.440
Forget Trump,

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:20.150
from a recent Vietnamese American

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:23.513
protest against these deportations.

00:36:24.886 --> 00:36:28.210
I think that with the
United States in many ways,

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:33.210
using Mexico as a place to
keep refugees right now,

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:37.360
we see the ways in which wealthy countries

00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:41.260
have acted to keep people
outside of their borders.

00:36:41.260 --> 00:36:43.370
I think that we need to
be a much more attention

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:45.400
to places like Mexico and Turkey,

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:48.840
that had been holding these
large numbers of refugees,

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:52.000
as we understand refugee politics today.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:53.080
So I've gone a little bit over,

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:54.590
but we have about 15, 20 minutes.

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:56.850
I hope people found that interesting.

00:36:56.850 --> 00:36:59.840
I would, love to hear
your questions or comments

00:36:59.840 --> 00:37:01.593
or arguments or critiques.

00:37:03.450 --> 00:37:05.130
- Thank you, Jana.

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:06.820
Take a moment (clapping) and applaud

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:08.493
this wonderful presentation.

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:14.253
I will take on the role of moderator,

00:37:15.570 --> 00:37:18.720
so that we'll allow Jana to
pay attention to the speakers

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:20.910
and the questions that are asked.

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:24.670
So what I propose is that
if you have a question,

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:28.040
you use the hand-raising function on Zoom.

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:31.330
You can go down in the participant section

00:37:31.330 --> 00:37:34.560
and raise your hand and I'll
try to keep track of you.

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:36.140
If for some reason you're having trouble

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:37.590
with the hand-raising function

00:37:37.590 --> 00:37:39.010
and you can't get my attention,

00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:41.213
you realize that I haven't seen you,

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:45.573
just please send a chat to
the everyone part of the chat,

00:37:46.790 --> 00:37:47.890
say that you have a question

00:37:47.890 --> 00:37:49.260
or you can even post the question

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:52.003
and then we'll allow Jana
to read it and answer.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:55.850
So lemme start by calling on anyone.

00:37:55.850 --> 00:37:58.410
Does anyone wanna start off our Q &amp; A?

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:00.960
Also please keep your
questions relatively short

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:03.900
so that we can allow as
many people as possible,

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:05.030
to ask questions.

00:38:05.030 --> 00:38:08.720
I should also add that
the event officially ends

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:10.680
at one o'clock, but I'll stick around,

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:13.720
I'll keep the Zoom window
open for another 15 minutes

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:16.203
after that if you have extra questions.

00:38:17.530 --> 00:38:18.743
Who would like to start?

00:38:19.812 --> 00:38:22.960
(door banging)

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:24.193
We'll start with Quan.

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:27.383
- Hi Jana.

00:38:27.383 --> 00:38:29.440
Thank you so much for your amazing talk

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.223
and congratulations on the book.

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:33.790
I was very struck...

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:36.810
so I just a book over the summer

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:41.810
and I thought the temporal
coverage of the book,

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:43.800
was really expansive

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:46.820
and it really takes our
understanding of refugees

00:38:46.820 --> 00:38:51.820
beyond ascending and host
country relationship.

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:54.810
And so I really think the
conversation about thinking

00:38:54.810 --> 00:38:59.550
about refugee camps as sites
that are often transit,

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:02.330
but here you're centreing these sites.

00:39:02.330 --> 00:39:03.780
So I'm wondering,

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:06.070
if you could say a little bit more about,

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.253
thinking of camp as an analytic,

00:39:09.320 --> 00:39:14.320
and how might, these
forgotten or abandoned sites,

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:18.330
help us re-centre
interstate relationships?

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:20.150
- Now, that is a wonderful question.

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:21.020
And thank you so much

00:39:21.020 --> 00:39:22.542
and thank you for your work as well.

00:39:22.542 --> 00:39:25.470
I just wanna tell you how
much I enjoy your work.

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:28.590
I guess for me, and this in
some ways it brings me back.

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.420
There's a way in which my work might seem

00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:33.260
to not make sense.

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:34.780
I wrote a book about Cuba,

00:39:34.780 --> 00:39:36.580
and now I'm bouncing
over to Southeast Asia.

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:38.400
And so what am I doing?

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:40.880
But the link actually is
the analytic of camps.

00:39:40.880 --> 00:39:43.120
Because if you think about the ways,

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:44.550
and this is how I'm thinking,

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:46.800
about the ways in which
I write about Guantanamo,

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:49.170
and I write about the Naval
base in Guantanamo Bay.

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:51.670
And I'm trying to think about

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:55.510
how do spaces that seem
peripheral and marginal

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:59.570
actually become central
to imperial politics.

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:02.240
I think that Empire's used places

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:04.660
that seem to be out of the way,

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:08.700
but in fact, magnify both
the extent of their power

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:10.470
and the limits, right?

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:13.343
And so I like playing with those tensions.

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:17.010
I think that there's a
way in which militaries

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:19.080
very clearly understand this.

00:40:19.080 --> 00:40:21.550
We think about where
military bases are located,

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:24.050
they understand the value of both being

00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:27.830
on the periphery of places
that might be missed,

00:40:27.830 --> 00:40:29.290
but yet seems still important.

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:31.670
I'm thinking about like
bases like Diego Garcia

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:33.300
and the Indian ocean,

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:35.540
or if we think about
where, their point of camp.

00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:38.100
So I think that there's a way

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:41.723
in which thinking more
about networks of power

00:40:41.723 --> 00:40:45.200
and the relationship between
empires and local communities,

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:47.240
that camps become places

00:40:47.240 --> 00:40:49.590
where those tensions become visible.

00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:51.220
Where we can see the ways

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:54.790
in which governments both
try to have state control,

00:40:54.790 --> 00:40:57.620
and then also in some ways
the limits of those control

00:40:57.620 --> 00:40:59.273
in a place like the camp.

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:01.890
I hope that helps answer your question,

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:04.040
but that's where I see my Guantanamo work

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:07.550
really in some ways
reverberating analytically,

00:41:07.550 --> 00:41:09.643
even if it's not there in the archives.

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:15.200
- Thanks for the great answer.

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:16.360
Next question will be from

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:19.053
Nu-Anh Tran at the
University of Connecticut.

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:22.573
- Hi, thank you for the presentation.

00:41:22.573 --> 00:41:24.340
That was really interesting.

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:25.550
My question is about

00:41:25.550 --> 00:41:30.373
how the politics of the refugees differ,

00:41:31.670 --> 00:41:33.510
due to the composition of the refugees.

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:36.300
You talked about how there are
more Northerners in Hong Kong

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:38.630
versus more Southerners in
some of the other camps.

00:41:38.630 --> 00:41:41.350
I was also thinking
that in the later years,

00:41:41.350 --> 00:41:42.183
there are more people

00:41:42.183 --> 00:41:44.090
who are actually just there
for like a short period of time

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:45.600
who are trying to think who was ODP

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:48.007
and not actually boat people.

00:41:48.007 --> 00:41:49.820
The other reason why I'm asking this is

00:41:49.820 --> 00:41:51.810
because I noticed that in
the picture from Hong Kong,

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:53.650
there are people using the RVN flag,

00:41:53.650 --> 00:41:55.210
the South Vietnamese flag.

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:58.320
So I was just wondering if like
the symbols or the arguments

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:02.930
or the tensions within
the refugee politics

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:04.040
differ by location

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:07.380
because of the composition
of the camp population.

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:09.130
- You know it's really good question

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:11.690
and I don't think my answer is as good.

00:42:11.690 --> 00:42:14.530
In some ways the South Vietnamese
flag is used repeatedly

00:42:14.530 --> 00:42:16.510
because I think it's a legible symbol.

00:42:16.510 --> 00:42:19.090
And so for the people protesting,

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:20.890
they want to be seen as refugees,

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:24.180
and so the South Vietnamese
flag is valuable in that way.

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:26.290
I'm not trying to underestimate

00:42:26.290 --> 00:42:27.510
their actual affinity with the flag.

00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:29.850
My guess is most of
the time it was strong.

00:42:29.850 --> 00:42:32.140
But it's a useful symbol.

00:42:32.140 --> 00:42:34.710
What I think is notable,

00:42:34.710 --> 00:42:36.340
and again, I'm looking
over to Carina on the side,

00:42:36.340 --> 00:42:37.820
but there's a lot of tension

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:39.210
between the North Vietnamese

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:40.270
and the South Vietnamese.

00:42:40.270 --> 00:42:42.170
Actually I think this is
an area for more research

00:42:42.170 --> 00:42:44.210
and this is where the fact
that I don't speak Vietnamese,

00:42:44.210 --> 00:42:45.043
I'll be really clear.

00:42:45.043 --> 00:42:47.960
I am not a Vietnamese language
speaker or reader matters,

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:49.250
and where I think someone
who does know Vietnamese

00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:50.273
could do more work.

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:54.557
A lot of time that there's anger

00:42:54.557 --> 00:42:56.607
and there's physical fighting
between the North Vietnamese

00:42:56.607 --> 00:42:59.000
and South Vietnamese
in the Hong Kong camps,

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:03.720
this is often attributed
to, regional tensions.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:06.380
I think what I think my hunch and again,

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:08.330
maybe some of the
Vietnamese could do more,

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:09.660
I think that's true.

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:12.990
I also think though that it is true

00:43:12.990 --> 00:43:16.960
that the North Vietnamese resented

00:43:16.960 --> 00:43:19.220
that the South Vietnamese
were seen as refugees.

00:43:19.220 --> 00:43:20.820
That it was easier if
you were South Vietnamese

00:43:20.820 --> 00:43:22.280
to get refugee status.

00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:24.330
So there were both
maybe lingering tensions

00:43:24.330 --> 00:43:26.770
vis-a-vis the actual
conflicts between North

00:43:26.770 --> 00:43:27.603
and South Vietnam,

00:43:27.603 --> 00:43:29.360
but there was also I think,

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:32.370
anger about who was seen as a refugee.

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:33.950
The other one I would only add is that,

00:43:33.950 --> 00:43:36.290
and I think, John Christianson Knutson

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:37.890
has written a great deal about this

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:39.440
and this idea of the screening machine

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:40.700
as Yen Leah Speartoov.

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:43.200
But there was a need to
tell a certain story,

00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:45.230
like in any refugee context.

00:43:45.230 --> 00:43:46.677
The right story was,

00:43:46.677 --> 00:43:49.537
"My father was in the
South Vietnamese Military,

00:43:49.537 --> 00:43:50.817
"I was discriminated against

00:43:50.817 --> 00:43:52.487
"because I was from South Vietnam.

00:43:52.487 --> 00:43:55.180
"My father was in a re-education camp."

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:58.160
That story was a story
that people would tell

00:43:58.160 --> 00:43:59.790
because it was effective.

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:01.740
What that does is it
minimized other stories.

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:02.573
So just quickly,

00:44:02.573 --> 00:44:04.550
one story that I really found emotive

00:44:04.550 --> 00:44:06.860
or compelling with one that didn't fit.

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:09.740
Which was a story of
a North Vietnamese man

00:44:09.740 --> 00:44:13.840
who went to East Germany
on a work program.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.290
When he was there, he got involved with

00:44:15.290 --> 00:44:17.000
independent union politics.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:18.650
So he gets deported from East Germany

00:44:18.650 --> 00:44:20.790
and sent back to North Vietnam.

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:22.200
Then he gets in problems there.

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:23.920
So they send him to Hong Kong.

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:27.120
He actually fit the definition
of an anti-communist refugee.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.100
But because he'd been to East Germany

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:32.630
and was from Northern Vietnam
and was a union activist,

00:44:32.630 --> 00:44:33.680
it didn't look right.

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:37.410
I think the symbols are
being used strategically

00:44:37.410 --> 00:44:38.243
as well sometimes,

00:44:38.243 --> 00:44:40.430
although not to underestimate

00:44:40.430 --> 00:44:42.130
people's actual affinities either.

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:45.740
Carina wants to jump in for a second.

00:44:47.720 --> 00:44:49.481
- Is it okay for me to say something now?

00:44:49.481 --> 00:44:50.314
- Yeah.

00:44:50.314 --> 00:44:53.020
Trinh, do you mind letting
Carina jump in real quick?

00:44:53.020 --> 00:44:53.853
Go ahead.

00:44:53.853 --> 00:44:55.140
- Thank you Trinh.

00:44:55.140 --> 00:44:58.610
- Just wanna add into this
particular conversation

00:44:58.610 --> 00:45:01.680
about North Vietnamese and
South Vietnamese in the camp.

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:05.120
I was in the refugee camp in Indonesia,

00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:07.460
and we didn't have this problem

00:45:07.460 --> 00:45:09.840
with Vietnamese fighting among each other

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:11.223
as much as Hong Kong.

00:45:12.370 --> 00:45:14.910
When you were talking
about the North Vietnamese

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:18.350
being resentful to a South Vietnamese,

00:45:18.350 --> 00:45:20.940
because they will not
consider as refugees,

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:23.810
and interestingly South
Vietnamese in the camp

00:45:23.810 --> 00:45:26.350
also resent the fact that
because of the North,

00:45:26.350 --> 00:45:28.590
they think because of the North Vietnamese

00:45:28.590 --> 00:45:33.590
that put them at risk of
getting refugee status.

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:37.280
Also the South Vietnamese who were stuck

00:45:37.280 --> 00:45:39.100
in the camp in Hong Kong,

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:41.950
blaming the North communist,
because of you guys,

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:44.780
now I lost country, I got to
escape and now I'm stuck here,

00:45:44.780 --> 00:45:46.240
so that's all they have.

00:45:46.240 --> 00:45:48.580
That political tension between them

00:45:48.580 --> 00:45:50.750
and of course different cultures,

00:45:50.750 --> 00:45:51.640
different values

00:45:51.640 --> 00:45:56.640
and the lifestyle North
Vietnamese and South Vietnamese

00:45:56.830 --> 00:45:58.450
are so different.

00:45:58.450 --> 00:46:00.610
Because of the fights
that they constantly have,

00:46:00.610 --> 00:46:03.110
it's actually put the Vietnamese refugees

00:46:03.110 --> 00:46:05.340
in a disadvantage situation,

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:08.600
because the Hong Kong
residents were saying that,

00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:12.130
we host you here, we provide you safety,

00:46:12.130 --> 00:46:13.570
we give everything to you,

00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:15.020
but you burned down the camp,

00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:17.260
you are causing problems.

00:46:17.260 --> 00:46:21.083
So eventually the Hong Kong residencies,

00:46:22.060 --> 00:46:24.510
they protest more toward the government

00:46:24.510 --> 00:46:27.330
and said, "Do not accept any
more Vietnamese refugees."

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:30.040
So that's another layer of pressure

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:32.920
that the Hong Kong
government had to deal with.

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:33.753
- Great.

00:46:33.753 --> 00:46:34.586
Thank you Carina.

00:46:35.660 --> 00:46:36.493
- Trinh.

00:46:37.900 --> 00:46:40.070
- Thank you so much for
your wonderful book talk

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:41.103
Professor Lipmann.

00:46:41.980 --> 00:46:44.530
I am thinking a lot about
what you said about Mexico,

00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:46.780
and I'm also thinking too,
like the camps in Europe,

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:48.730
like for example, Moria
which just burnt down

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:49.970
to two weeks ago.

00:46:49.970 --> 00:46:52.880
It never really occurred
to me to think of camps

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:55.960
in like Wan in Hong Kong as
sites of like liminality,

00:46:55.960 --> 00:46:57.720
but also agency.

00:46:57.720 --> 00:47:00.230
But the thing is the
reception of refugees,

00:47:00.230 --> 00:47:02.210
like Vietnamese refugees in Southeast Asia

00:47:02.210 --> 00:47:04.080
and "cold war" refugees
was quite different

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:06.160
than what's going on today.

00:47:06.160 --> 00:47:07.710
I know that's just one facet of it.

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:11.110
So, thinking to your work
across countries like in Cuba,

00:47:11.110 --> 00:47:15.080
how do you understand the
difference in response

00:47:15.080 --> 00:47:19.923
to efforts to self advocate
for resettlement and such?

00:47:22.124 --> 00:47:24.400
- I think that's exactly right.

00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:27.223
I don't wanna be nostalgic
for the cold war.

00:47:28.340 --> 00:47:30.590
But on the other hand,

00:47:30.590 --> 00:47:34.300
the cold war put pressure

00:47:34.300 --> 00:47:36.070
on places like the United
States and Great Britain

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:37.390
to accept people

00:47:37.390 --> 00:47:39.440
in a way that our current government

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:41.813
does not feel external pressure to do so.

00:47:43.070 --> 00:47:45.500
I think that we are dealing with,

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:48.010
far more fraught situations in some ways

00:47:48.010 --> 00:47:52.400
than Cubans or Vietnamese
did in the 70s, 80s and 90s.

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:55.930
I think when I was doing my research...

00:47:55.930 --> 00:47:57.650
and very critical of the
Hong Kong government,

00:47:57.650 --> 00:47:59.480
in the book in multiple ways,

00:47:59.480 --> 00:48:00.980
although I also am somewhat sympathetic

00:48:00.980 --> 00:48:02.850
to the pressures that they faced,

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:06.340
and there's a way in which
they keep being worried

00:48:06.340 --> 00:48:07.930
that if they push people off,

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:09.890
people won't think of them
as being humanitarian.

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:11.380
They're supposed to be British.

00:48:11.380 --> 00:48:12.600
They can't do X

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:15.320
because they have to be
seen as humanitarian.

00:48:15.320 --> 00:48:16.227
I was like, "Oh my goodness.

00:48:16.227 --> 00:48:20.387
"If only our countries still
felt pressure to look good,

00:48:20.387 --> 00:48:22.560
"things would be at least
a little bit better."

00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:24.300
And that sort of need

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:27.850
to at least have a facade
of humanitarianism,

00:48:27.850 --> 00:48:29.780
even that is gone as gone.

00:48:29.780 --> 00:48:34.780
I think that that eraser
or political stance

00:48:35.271 --> 00:48:36.900
that we don't even need to pretend

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:38.800
to be protecting human rights anymore,

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:43.440
is a huge shift from the cold
war the post cold war age.

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:45.400
That a country like the United States,

00:48:45.400 --> 00:48:48.230
and arguably even
countries in Western Europe

00:48:48.230 --> 00:48:50.830
no longer even have to pretend

00:48:50.830 --> 00:48:53.690
that they care about human
rights and humanitarianism.

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:55.530
In a way that some of the pressures,

00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:58.090
both of colonialism on England,

00:48:58.090 --> 00:49:02.270
as well as the United
States where the cold war

00:49:02.270 --> 00:49:04.070
created at least a need

00:49:04.070 --> 00:49:07.090
to pay a little bit more than lip service.

00:49:08.815 --> 00:49:10.683
That is no longer even the case.

00:49:12.381 --> 00:49:13.760
I think the situation
right now is really grim.

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:16.687
I think the Vietnamese...

00:49:16.687 --> 00:49:17.920
and many of them were sent back.

00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:19.790
I'm not again trying
to underestimate that.

00:49:19.790 --> 00:49:23.360
But today people have even fewer avenues

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:26.410
because the legal system
has been so monopolized

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:29.143
by conservative administrative judges.

00:49:34.100 --> 00:49:36.000
- It's time for a question from Peter.

00:49:38.992 --> 00:49:39.930
(door banging)

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:42.720
Professor Lipmann, I have a question

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:45.593
in regards to the Malaysian case example.

00:49:47.820 --> 00:49:52.090
At the time, were there
local Chinese Malay,

00:49:52.090 --> 00:49:54.790
political parties, organizations, bodies,

00:49:54.790 --> 00:49:56.800
whatsoever and what have you,

00:49:56.800 --> 00:50:00.494
who advocated for these "Chinese"

00:50:00.494 --> 00:50:02.317
coming into the country or did they say,

00:50:02.317 --> 00:50:05.300
"Oh, no, we don't want
anything to do with them?"

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:07.490
- So that's a really good question.

00:50:07.490 --> 00:50:09.700
I tried to do some research on that

00:50:09.700 --> 00:50:11.770
and I found less than
I would have thought.

00:50:11.770 --> 00:50:14.260
But even that silence
I think is interesting.

00:50:14.260 --> 00:50:15.680
I would again say that's an area

00:50:15.680 --> 00:50:16.990
where I think there
should be more research.

00:50:16.990 --> 00:50:19.180
And again, with someone
whose language skills

00:50:19.180 --> 00:50:20.450
are better than mine.

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:22.180
I was in Malaysia for several weeks,

00:50:22.180 --> 00:50:23.700
I had a research translator

00:50:25.001 --> 00:50:26.280
who could speak both Chinese and Malay.

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:28.320
So we looked at a lot
of Chinese newspapers

00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:30.530
and Chinese Malaysian newspapers

00:50:30.530 --> 00:50:32.910
to see what were the Chinese
political parties saying.

00:50:32.910 --> 00:50:33.743
Right?

00:50:33.743 --> 00:50:35.620
'Cause there are several
Chinese political parties

00:50:35.620 --> 00:50:36.610
in Malaysia,

00:50:36.610 --> 00:50:38.027
some that were affiliated
with the government

00:50:38.027 --> 00:50:40.280
and some of them were in the opposition.

00:50:40.280 --> 00:50:42.240
There was actually remarkably little

00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:45.463
in the Chinese language
newspapers at the time.

00:50:47.070 --> 00:50:50.040
In fact, the Malay newspapers commented

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:52.970
on the Chinese language
newspapers' silence.

00:50:52.970 --> 00:50:57.250
It seems to be that there was a reluctance

00:50:57.250 --> 00:51:00.210
to support the Chinese Vietnamese

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:03.465
because it would have been
politically unpopular.

00:51:03.465 --> 00:51:06.140
They were in some ways concerned

00:51:06.140 --> 00:51:08.760
about not being seen
as nationalists enough.

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:09.593
Right.

00:51:09.593 --> 00:51:11.640
They wanna be seen as being
Malaysian nationalists

00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:14.653
and not specific towards
the Chinese Vietnamese.

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:20.170
There are leaders who do
talk about humanitarianism.

00:51:20.170 --> 00:51:22.560
They are often against
the government's desire

00:51:22.560 --> 00:51:24.550
to push people off.

00:51:24.550 --> 00:51:26.650
But I would say there was actually much...

00:51:26.650 --> 00:51:28.050
I thought there was gonna be a lot more

00:51:28.050 --> 00:51:31.160
in the Chinese language
newspapers in Malaysia,

00:51:31.160 --> 00:51:32.600
in fact, there was a lot more

00:51:32.600 --> 00:51:34.720
in the Malay language newspapers,

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:36.290
than there was in the Chinese.

00:51:36.290 --> 00:51:37.470
I would only add,

00:51:37.470 --> 00:51:39.030
and this is where someone
like Carina's working.

00:51:39.030 --> 00:51:41.520
There are other oral history collections.

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:43.500
What I did find instead though,

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:46.650
was in Vietnamese oral histories,

00:51:46.650 --> 00:51:50.007
people would say, "Oh,
a Chinese Malaysian,

00:51:50.007 --> 00:51:53.040
"met me on the shore and helped me out."

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:56.157
Or, "Oh, so-and-so met me

00:51:56.157 --> 00:51:59.140
"and they helped me get
to the right place."

00:51:59.140 --> 00:52:02.780
There seemed to be at a personal level,

00:52:02.780 --> 00:52:06.040
some connections and
they often both spoke,

00:52:06.040 --> 00:52:07.930
sometimes it's shared to Chinese language,

00:52:07.930 --> 00:52:08.763
sometimes it not.

00:52:08.763 --> 00:52:10.190
Malaysians speak,

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:12.840
generally speak a
different Chinese dialect

00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:15.060
and then Cantonese.

00:52:15.060 --> 00:52:15.893
But in any case,

00:52:15.893 --> 00:52:18.590
there were some moments where
there in the oral histories,

00:52:18.590 --> 00:52:21.130
these moments of connection
would come together.

00:52:21.130 --> 00:52:25.110
But I found that the
Chinese language press

00:52:25.110 --> 00:52:27.810
was much quieter on this
topic than I had anticipated.

00:52:31.780 --> 00:52:33.513
- We'll turn to Tiffany.

00:52:38.690 --> 00:52:41.463
Tiffany you need to unmute
briefly you're still muted.

00:52:43.210 --> 00:52:44.620
- Alright.

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:47.180
Well I was saying that
thanks so much to Jana

00:52:47.180 --> 00:52:49.390
for putting together such a wonderful book

00:52:49.390 --> 00:52:53.440
that provides invaluable resources

00:52:53.440 --> 00:52:54.940
for those of us who want to learn

00:52:54.940 --> 00:52:56.733
about this exodus history.

00:52:58.870 --> 00:53:03.610
I came across at the UNHCR archives.

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:05.690
I came across a number of documents

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:10.690
talking about people in
1975 wanted to repatriate,

00:53:10.760 --> 00:53:13.450
but I never really followed up on that.

00:53:13.450 --> 00:53:16.680
But it's so wonderful and really exciting

00:53:18.170 --> 00:53:19.290
to actually read your book

00:53:19.290 --> 00:53:21.993
and learn about that in more detail.

00:53:23.184 --> 00:53:24.320
I also appreciate

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:29.470
the way you navigate the
sensitivity of terminology,

00:53:29.470 --> 00:53:33.740
especially whether to call
all Vietnamese refugees

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:38.690
as a lot of humanitarian
groups would advocate for.

00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:42.480
For me it just the sake of
identifying different periods

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:46.630
and how policy changes in
Southeast Asia, in Hong Kong

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:48.560
and with the UNHCR as well,

00:53:48.560 --> 00:53:50.343
that we have to make that clear.

00:53:51.474 --> 00:53:54.430
I really liked the way you navigated that.

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:56.510
Now my question is,

00:53:56.510 --> 00:54:00.000
in terms of the title, "In Camps,"

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:02.310
I understand that in Vietnamese language,

00:54:02.310 --> 00:54:05.930
they call everything 'Trai' means 'camp.'

00:54:05.930 --> 00:54:08.563
but with the case of Hong Kong,

00:54:09.930 --> 00:54:13.070
I was with a group of Vietnamese

00:54:13.070 --> 00:54:16.110
that got resettled in
Hong Kong for three years,

00:54:16.110 --> 00:54:19.140
and I listened to the
way they used the terms,

00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:22.190
and it has always been very separate.

00:54:22.190 --> 00:54:26.440
Because there were a
number of detention centres

00:54:26.440 --> 00:54:29.230
that hosted Vietnamese refugees.

00:54:29.230 --> 00:54:31.550
This is including Victoria prison

00:54:31.550 --> 00:54:34.380
that turned into Tai Kwun contemporary art

00:54:34.380 --> 00:54:37.523
that I worked at like few years ago.

00:54:38.940 --> 00:54:40.977
Most of them call 'giam tam',

00:54:42.000 --> 00:54:43.880
to refer to detention centre

00:54:43.880 --> 00:54:47.200
and 'trai ti nan' to refugee camp.

00:54:47.200 --> 00:54:52.200
'Giam tam' also, I think
the term came about in 1982,

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:56.690
when Hong Kong started to
enforce close camp policy,

00:54:56.690 --> 00:54:59.470
even admitting Vietnamese as refugees,

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:02.840
there was still like close camp policies,

00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:05.350
so the Vietnamese would not be able

00:55:05.350 --> 00:55:07.190
to move around freely.

00:55:07.190 --> 00:55:10.400
So how did you navigate around

00:55:10.400 --> 00:55:12.350
this kind of like limelight

00:55:12.350 --> 00:55:15.160
to come up with a title In Camp?

00:55:15.160 --> 00:55:16.710
- That's a really good question

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:17.930
and in some ways I think

00:55:17.930 --> 00:55:20.600
is also a really valuable critique.

00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:21.850
I guess I would say a few things.

00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:25.340
One is that you are,
first of all, correct.

00:55:25.340 --> 00:55:27.280
They're not all camps,

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:29.525
they don't have barbed wire...

00:55:29.525 --> 00:55:31.060
In Pulau there's no barbed wire,

00:55:31.060 --> 00:55:31.893
even a lot of the times.

00:55:31.893 --> 00:55:32.726
By then the Philippines,

00:55:32.726 --> 00:55:35.690
these are open places where
people would work outside

00:55:35.690 --> 00:55:37.290
and it was very different.

00:55:37.290 --> 00:55:40.260
I think that I chose
the title in some ways,

00:55:40.260 --> 00:55:42.010
because I wanted to be
raising the questions

00:55:42.010 --> 00:55:47.010
that Quan asked earlier
about the idea of the camp.

00:55:47.490 --> 00:55:50.230
Thinking about these spaces where people

00:55:50.230 --> 00:55:54.420
were before resettlement
and after they left.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:55.940
And so I think that you're right.

00:55:55.940 --> 00:56:00.940
That in some ways it may be
too simplistic or catchy,

00:56:03.029 --> 00:56:03.862
but in surveys lines,

00:56:03.862 --> 00:56:06.860
these are larger linguistic
and political ideas.

00:56:06.860 --> 00:56:09.050
But the reason I guess I landed on it

00:56:09.050 --> 00:56:12.020
because it was able to bring together

00:56:12.020 --> 00:56:13.770
all the multiple stories

00:56:13.770 --> 00:56:16.150
that I was trying to tell
from Guam to Malaysia,

00:56:16.150 --> 00:56:18.150
to Hong Kong, to the Philippines.

00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:21.030
And even if it is not as precise a term

00:56:21.030 --> 00:56:23.650
as potentially, it should be,

00:56:23.650 --> 00:56:26.390
it allowed me to make the
argument and the title

00:56:26.390 --> 00:56:28.110
that the book is about

00:56:28.110 --> 00:56:31.180
why we must think about
these places in between

00:56:31.180 --> 00:56:32.690
and why that is necessary

00:56:32.690 --> 00:56:35.050
for actually understanding
refugee politics.

00:56:35.050 --> 00:56:38.950
So I take your points really seriously,

00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:40.600
but I guess from my point of view,

00:56:40.600 --> 00:56:43.020
it did some of the analytical work

00:56:43.020 --> 00:56:47.320
that I wanted it to do by
bringing the stories together.

00:56:47.320 --> 00:56:48.390
- I think there's a good,

00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:51.390
like all encompassing kind of term,

00:56:51.390 --> 00:56:52.720
like when you use the word camp,

00:56:52.720 --> 00:56:55.300
because that when you can also critique

00:56:55.300 --> 00:56:57.790
these refugee places

00:56:57.790 --> 00:57:00.600
are actually military bases and camps.

00:57:00.600 --> 00:57:03.210
So I do see the point.

00:57:03.210 --> 00:57:04.043
Thank you.

00:57:04.043 --> 00:57:04.950
- Yeah, no, thank you.

00:57:07.550 --> 00:57:08.630
- Great question.

00:57:08.630 --> 00:57:11.620
I wanna flag to everyone
that it's one o'clock

00:57:11.620 --> 00:57:13.170
for those of you who had appointments

00:57:13.170 --> 00:57:14.003
and needed to leave,

00:57:14.003 --> 00:57:16.423
but we'll go into extra time,

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:20.240
as if it's a exciting soccer match.

00:57:20.240 --> 00:57:22.580
So we'll go into extra time now,

00:57:22.580 --> 00:57:25.033
and I have a question from Ivan Small.

00:57:26.290 --> 00:57:27.570
So Ivan.

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:29.330
- Thanks Jana for really
a interesting talk

00:57:29.330 --> 00:57:30.650
and for an interesting book.

00:57:30.650 --> 00:57:33.050
I've picked it up and started reading it.

00:57:33.050 --> 00:57:34.100
I just had a quick question.

00:57:34.100 --> 00:57:35.073
I was curious.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:39.460
Are there many people in
the Vietnamese diaspora

00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:42.493
that are interested in
returning to these camps?

00:57:43.610 --> 00:57:47.730
And, using this camp as a
site of collective memory?

00:57:47.730 --> 00:57:51.280
You mentioned, help and Pulau

00:57:51.280 --> 00:57:54.800
and there was kind of an
effort there under Father Vien,

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:56.810
but it seemed like that didn't work out.

00:57:56.810 --> 00:57:59.690
So I was wondering if
there are concerted efforts

00:57:59.690 --> 00:58:01.460
right now to do that.

00:58:01.460 --> 00:58:03.630
- This is where Quan
has done much more work

00:58:03.630 --> 00:58:04.880
than I have.

00:58:04.880 --> 00:58:08.320
But no, there not to live by any stretch

00:58:08.320 --> 00:58:09.153
of the imagination.

00:58:09.153 --> 00:58:11.950
But as far as pilgrimage sites,

00:58:11.950 --> 00:58:13.402
there are large numbers,

00:58:13.402 --> 00:58:14.235
or I don't know what,

00:58:14.235 --> 00:58:16.570
they're sizeable numbers
of Vietnamese in diaspora,

00:58:16.570 --> 00:58:18.330
United States, Canada, Australia,

00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:22.320
who have gone back to these
places as pilgrimage sites.

00:58:22.320 --> 00:58:24.190
And Carina has also brought people

00:58:24.190 --> 00:58:27.023
to the camps in former sites in Indonesia.

00:58:29.170 --> 00:58:31.620
There's a way in which...

00:58:31.620 --> 00:58:33.520
so for example, when I was in Malaysia

00:58:35.240 --> 00:58:36.180
they're temples,

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:39.120
there's statues that are maintained

00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:41.140
by diasporic groups,

00:58:41.140 --> 00:58:44.080
the man who took me to Pulau Bidong,

00:58:44.080 --> 00:58:47.253
takes Vietnamese Australian
groups regularly.

00:58:50.797 --> 00:58:51.667
Many people died in these camps

00:58:51.667 --> 00:58:54.730
and in some ways these camps are places,

00:58:54.730 --> 00:58:57.040
where people lost people.

00:58:57.040 --> 00:58:57.980
But there is,

00:58:57.980 --> 00:59:00.010
I think a sense of return,

00:59:00.010 --> 00:59:03.673
there's large numbers of religious
memorials in these camps.

00:59:05.840 --> 00:59:09.800
I think that there's not any
interest in resettlement,

00:59:09.800 --> 00:59:12.220
like Father Vien was about
bringing Vietnamese Americans

00:59:12.220 --> 00:59:14.140
to help out at the camps, right.

00:59:14.140 --> 00:59:15.240
To bringing volunteers.

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:17.500
And then you have this, vision.

00:59:17.500 --> 00:59:19.400
This is in the Philippines,

00:59:19.400 --> 00:59:21.913
there was an idea that Philippines
does not deport people.

00:59:21.913 --> 00:59:24.700
That Philippines, resettles
a group of Vietnamese

00:59:24.700 --> 00:59:26.040
in the Philippines.

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:27.060
In the end that group

00:59:27.060 --> 00:59:28.860
doesn't actually wanna
stay in the Philippines,

00:59:28.860 --> 00:59:31.430
most of them also want to
come to the United States

00:59:31.430 --> 00:59:34.223
and eventually do as late as 2005.

00:59:35.060 --> 00:59:38.590
But Vietnamese Americans
more, have gone...

00:59:40.340 --> 00:59:41.880
I would think the word pilgrimage

00:59:41.880 --> 00:59:43.010
is probably appropriate,

00:59:43.010 --> 00:59:44.520
but if Quan or Carina

00:59:44.520 --> 00:59:47.513
want to have a different nuance on it,

00:59:48.360 --> 00:59:49.690
be interested in your thoughts.

00:59:49.690 --> 00:59:51.300
But not as far as resettling,

00:59:51.300 --> 00:59:52.913
but as far as they are trips.

00:59:54.920 --> 00:59:56.770
Does that answer your question, Ivan?

00:59:58.310 --> 00:59:59.451
- Yeah, thank you.

00:59:59.451 --> 01:00:00.668
- Okay.

01:00:00.668 --> 01:00:02.160
I don't know if Carina wanna add anything.

01:00:02.160 --> 01:00:02.993
- Thanks Jana.

01:00:02.993 --> 01:00:04.272
Can I add to that?

01:00:04.272 --> 01:00:05.105
It's just also,

01:00:05.105 --> 01:00:08.620
in terms of what became
of these camps after

01:00:08.620 --> 01:00:10.723
resettlement or repatriation.

01:00:11.744 --> 01:00:14.800
In the cases of Indonesia
and Malaysia, for example,

01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:17.630
some of those camps or
remnants of those camps

01:00:17.630 --> 01:00:19.180
continue to be there,

01:00:19.180 --> 01:00:20.780
and they've actually turned into like

01:00:20.780 --> 01:00:22.070
tourist destinations

01:00:23.045 --> 01:00:25.650
for not just the refugees
who are returning

01:00:25.650 --> 01:00:27.580
to visit as pilgrimage sites,

01:00:27.580 --> 01:00:30.150
but also for local populations

01:00:30.150 --> 01:00:32.150
and the kind of laws that are associated

01:00:32.150 --> 01:00:35.140
now with these forlorn
places in some ways.

01:00:35.140 --> 01:00:38.670
In the case of Hong Kong,
or even in Thailand,

01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:39.780
the traces of those camps

01:00:39.780 --> 01:00:42.680
are no longer physically visible.

01:00:42.680 --> 01:00:45.210
You really have to dig for it.

01:00:45.210 --> 01:00:47.331
There's no concerted effort

01:00:47.331 --> 01:00:51.070
to preserve these sites, on a local level.

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:54.070
That also impact the ability of people

01:00:54.070 --> 01:00:59.070
to go back and think about
on individual levels.

01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:02.970
So there are people who
do go back as individuals,

01:01:02.970 --> 01:01:05.323
there are organizations in the diaspora,

01:01:06.222 --> 01:01:10.150
that are kind of spearheading
some of these pilgrimage tours

01:01:10.150 --> 01:01:10.983
and so on.

01:01:14.900 --> 01:01:16.838
- Carina, please join in.

01:01:16.838 --> 01:01:17.890
- Yes.

01:01:17.890 --> 01:01:19.630
In terms of Indonesia,

01:01:19.630 --> 01:01:23.093
sort of unique than the
other places, in two ways.

01:01:24.440 --> 01:01:27.050
The main refugee camp in phosphoric,

01:01:27.050 --> 01:01:29.258
became a tourist attraction place,

01:01:29.258 --> 01:01:32.563
mainly for Vietnamese who return to visit.

01:01:33.540 --> 01:01:36.880
But for places where I
would like Co To Island,

01:01:36.880 --> 01:01:41.390
where I first landed, these
were uninhabited islands.

01:01:41.390 --> 01:01:44.810
So initially there were
hundreds of Vietnamese refugees

01:01:44.810 --> 01:01:48.130
died as soon as we arrived
in the first few months,

01:01:48.130 --> 01:01:50.127
so more and more Vietnamese people

01:01:50.127 --> 01:01:51.903
return to these places,

01:01:52.918 --> 01:01:55.773
to look for the graves
of their loved ones.

01:01:57.310 --> 01:01:59.500
A lot of my work for refugees

01:01:59.500 --> 01:02:02.340
focus on these in uninhabited islands,

01:02:02.340 --> 01:02:05.223
just mainly looking
for graves of refugees.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:09.310
- I would also add to Quan's point

01:02:09.310 --> 01:02:11.240
in the Philippines it's very different.

01:02:11.240 --> 01:02:12.073
Maybe not that different,

01:02:12.073 --> 01:02:14.760
but the main camp in the
Philippines was in Bataan,

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:16.980
which is the Philippine
Refugee Processing Center.

01:02:16.980 --> 01:02:18.390
That is not part of my talk.

01:02:18.390 --> 01:02:19.640
It was very different.

01:02:19.640 --> 01:02:22.410
The Philippines Refugee Processing Center

01:02:22.410 --> 01:02:26.580
was for people who had already
been given a refugee status.

01:02:26.580 --> 01:02:27.507
The United States said,

01:02:27.507 --> 01:02:28.657
"Well, before you come to America,

01:02:28.657 --> 01:02:30.627
"it will be cheaper for
you to learn English

01:02:30.627 --> 01:02:32.347
"in the Philippines for six months,

01:02:32.347 --> 01:02:34.760
"and then you'll come
to the United States."

01:02:34.760 --> 01:02:36.900
And so people there
knew they were gonna get

01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:38.330
to the United States or Germany,

01:02:38.330 --> 01:02:40.040
or Norway, is a few people.

01:02:40.040 --> 01:02:42.730
But that now is a museum.

01:02:42.730 --> 01:02:43.563
It's somewhat different

01:02:43.563 --> 01:02:44.660
because the Philippine government

01:02:44.660 --> 01:02:47.250
is very proud of that project.

01:02:47.250 --> 01:02:49.740
Some of it actually is also fallen down

01:02:49.740 --> 01:02:51.210
because they don't have
the hundreds of thousands

01:02:51.210 --> 01:02:52.640
of people there anymore,

01:02:52.640 --> 01:02:54.653
but there's also a museum there.

01:02:55.657 --> 01:02:56.620
Avenna Speartov

01:02:56.620 --> 01:02:58.880
who also does a lot of this work

01:02:58.880 --> 01:03:00.923
has done a film about the PRPC camp.

01:03:06.361 --> 01:03:08.370
- If there's no objection,

01:03:08.370 --> 01:03:10.163
I'd like to ask a question too.

01:03:11.860 --> 01:03:14.860
I just wanted to add before
asking my own question,

01:03:14.860 --> 01:03:17.670
the article I mentioned
that Quan had written

01:03:17.670 --> 01:03:19.710
also adds to your story

01:03:19.710 --> 01:03:22.570
about the politics within Southeast Asia

01:03:22.570 --> 01:03:24.370
that takes place during the refugee,

01:03:24.370 --> 01:03:27.170
but also the politics of memorialization.

01:03:27.170 --> 01:03:28.610
There's some pretty dramatic stories

01:03:28.610 --> 01:03:31.463
that Quan relays in that about,

01:03:32.398 --> 01:03:34.510
the Vietnamese government,

01:03:34.510 --> 01:03:37.270
interacting with Asian and
some of the various countries

01:03:37.270 --> 01:03:39.567
in order to remove memorials,

01:03:39.567 --> 01:03:41.980
and the kind of political
pressures that are going

01:03:41.980 --> 01:03:42.813
on there as well,

01:03:42.813 --> 01:03:44.450
which is quite fascinating.

01:03:44.450 --> 01:03:47.170
So the story you tell is quite...

01:03:47.170 --> 01:03:52.170
it lingers into the post-exodus period.

01:03:52.700 --> 01:03:53.970
I wanna ask a question,

01:03:53.970 --> 01:03:56.740
that's kind of a little bit about

01:03:56.740 --> 01:03:58.103
how you wrote the book.

01:03:59.450 --> 01:04:00.840
I'll preface it a little bit

01:04:00.840 --> 01:04:02.420
for those who haven't read it yet.

01:04:02.420 --> 01:04:05.518
But as you heard from the talk everyone,

01:04:05.518 --> 01:04:09.780
the book is organized
sort of by geographically,

01:04:09.780 --> 01:04:11.200
but at the same time...

01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:12.540
so we have the different places.

01:04:12.540 --> 01:04:14.179
We have Malaysia,

01:04:14.179 --> 01:04:15.050
we have Guam,

01:04:15.050 --> 01:04:16.590
Starts with Guam, then there's Malaysia,

01:04:16.590 --> 01:04:18.743
then there's the
Philippines and Hong Kong.

01:04:19.920 --> 01:04:22.470
It gives this wonderful geographic spread

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:24.410
of the inter-Asian politics

01:04:24.410 --> 01:04:25.730
and the different politics

01:04:25.730 --> 01:04:27.430
of the different places.

01:04:27.430 --> 01:04:28.400
But at the same time,

01:04:28.400 --> 01:04:30.300
this is my question.

01:04:30.300 --> 01:04:31.560
At the same time, Jana,

01:04:31.560 --> 01:04:35.010
you layer kind of a
historical chronology on top.

01:04:35.010 --> 01:04:37.920
So it both goes kind of geographically,

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:39.670
but also goes historically.

01:04:39.670 --> 01:04:44.190
So we start in Guam is for '75.

01:04:44.190 --> 01:04:47.010
Malaysia is '75 through '79,

01:04:47.010 --> 01:04:50.820
and that ends up in the
UNHCR conference in '79

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:51.993
that's very important.

01:04:53.410 --> 01:04:54.660
There's the Bataan

01:04:54.660 --> 01:04:57.600
which over-intersects with people power

01:04:57.600 --> 01:05:00.500
in the Marcos regime and
Aquino and all this stuff.

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:04.810
And then there's, 1980
through '89 in Hong Kong,

01:05:04.810 --> 01:05:06.770
which tells a different
kind of political story,

01:05:06.770 --> 01:05:10.710
which ends up with this shift
that leads to screening.

01:05:10.710 --> 01:05:12.830
So my question for you as a writer,

01:05:12.830 --> 01:05:15.280
and as a historian,

01:05:15.280 --> 01:05:17.350
how did you make that decision?

01:05:17.350 --> 01:05:20.480
Because the camps don't necessarily stop

01:05:20.480 --> 01:05:23.257
at the end of the period that you choose.

01:05:23.257 --> 01:05:24.503
I was just curious,

01:05:26.130 --> 01:05:27.750
what were some of your other options

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:30.730
for structuring the book
as you were imagining it,

01:05:30.730 --> 01:05:32.150
and how you settled on this

01:05:32.150 --> 01:05:34.130
and maybe some of the
challenges that you faced

01:05:34.130 --> 01:05:35.330
as you made that choice?

01:05:36.347 --> 01:05:37.180
- Thank you so much.

01:05:37.180 --> 01:05:39.133
Thanks so much for reading it so closely.

01:05:40.830 --> 01:05:43.313
It's a sprawling book.

01:05:45.590 --> 01:05:46.423
You're right.

01:05:46.423 --> 01:05:48.390
There's a way in which I stopped worrying

01:05:48.390 --> 01:05:49.930
about Malaysia in 1979,

01:05:49.930 --> 01:05:51.160
but of course there are Vietnamese

01:05:51.160 --> 01:05:52.800
in Malaysia till the mid 90s.

01:05:52.800 --> 01:05:53.633
Right?

01:05:54.660 --> 01:05:57.390
So it doesn't quite work that way.

01:05:57.390 --> 01:06:00.880
I guess I decided that there were...

01:06:03.020 --> 01:06:04.230
as soon as I wrote my first book,

01:06:04.230 --> 01:06:05.063
somebody's like this.

01:06:05.063 --> 01:06:07.870
That I like thinking both
thematically and chronologically.

01:06:07.870 --> 01:06:08.840
I was trying to figure out,

01:06:08.840 --> 01:06:11.950
which were the stories I wanted to tell.

01:06:11.950 --> 01:06:13.300
And where was the points?

01:06:13.300 --> 01:06:16.540
Where were the places where
those questions sort of emerged?

01:06:16.540 --> 01:06:18.560
I guess archivally what I did

01:06:18.560 --> 01:06:20.540
is I had found all this stuff...

01:06:20.540 --> 01:06:21.780
lemme talk a little backwards.

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:24.620
I found all of the material about Guam.

01:06:24.620 --> 01:06:25.483
I was like, "This is an amazing story.

01:06:25.483 --> 01:06:27.000
"I'm gonna run with it."

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:27.833
And then after that,

01:06:27.833 --> 01:06:28.750
I didn't really know what I was doing.

01:06:28.750 --> 01:06:30.330
And I didn't know much
about Southeast Asia.

01:06:30.330 --> 01:06:32.820
I didn't even know about
the 1989 conference

01:06:32.820 --> 01:06:34.030
or '79 on conference,

01:06:34.030 --> 01:06:36.120
but I went to Geneva.

01:06:36.120 --> 01:06:38.370
Geneva, the UNHCR archives.

01:06:38.370 --> 01:06:40.190
I was like, I'm gonna start here.

01:06:40.190 --> 01:06:42.920
And I'm gonna start by
looking at the Geneva archives

01:06:42.920 --> 01:06:45.740
and from there deciding
where I was going to go.

01:06:45.740 --> 01:06:47.830
So other factics of my book,

01:06:47.830 --> 01:06:48.950
I don't deal with Thailand,

01:06:48.950 --> 01:06:50.530
which was actually
quite important as well,

01:06:50.530 --> 01:06:51.560
but it's more complicated

01:06:51.560 --> 01:06:54.460
'cause you had so many more
people from Cambodia and Laos.

01:06:55.300 --> 01:06:58.650
But there's a way that I started
with the Geneva archives.

01:06:58.650 --> 01:07:00.110
Those archives led me to that,

01:07:00.110 --> 01:07:03.450
Oh Malaysia is what they're
worried about in the '70s.

01:07:03.450 --> 01:07:05.891
Then Hong Kong is what
they're worried about

01:07:05.891 --> 01:07:07.860
in the '80s and '90s.

01:07:07.860 --> 01:07:09.490
And so in many ways,

01:07:09.490 --> 01:07:11.930
I used that initial trip to Geneva

01:07:11.930 --> 01:07:14.070
and the UNHCRs archives

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:17.360
to think about what is the map,

01:07:17.360 --> 01:07:20.283
both geographically and chronologically.

01:07:21.270 --> 01:07:23.930
In some ways you could argue

01:07:23.930 --> 01:07:26.030
that going to Geneva first,

01:07:26.030 --> 01:07:27.310
it's going to the capital

01:07:27.310 --> 01:07:28.940
rather than going to the sort of,

01:07:28.940 --> 01:07:30.880
as Quan was asking the provinces, right?

01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:32.603
Instead of going then
starting with the camps,

01:07:32.603 --> 01:07:34.220
then going inward,

01:07:34.220 --> 01:07:36.540
I started with the capital archives

01:07:36.540 --> 01:07:37.870
and went outward.

01:07:37.870 --> 01:07:41.340
But that allowed me to start thinking

01:07:41.340 --> 01:07:44.640
about the landscape of the book.

01:07:44.640 --> 01:07:46.760
And, from there,

01:07:46.760 --> 01:07:49.700
I just had to make decisions
about where and when,

01:07:49.700 --> 01:07:51.280
and I just decided

01:07:51.280 --> 01:07:53.860
that I didn't have to tell the
whole story about Malaysia.

01:07:53.860 --> 01:07:55.480
That it was gonna be okay.

01:07:55.480 --> 01:07:57.430
That the stories in Hong Kong

01:07:57.430 --> 01:08:00.320
were more illustrative of
that moment of screening,

01:08:00.320 --> 01:08:03.170
even though there was screening
in Malaysia or Indonesia.

01:08:04.530 --> 01:08:05.656
- Great.

01:08:05.656 --> 01:08:06.489
- I hope that answers your question.

01:08:06.489 --> 01:08:07.322
- No it really does.

01:08:07.322 --> 01:08:09.100
And I wanna just respond by saying it

01:08:09.100 --> 01:08:10.380
wasn't a critique so much,

01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:13.550
as I was actually thought it
was a very elegant solution

01:08:13.550 --> 01:08:17.300
to telling both the chronological
and geographical story

01:08:17.300 --> 01:08:19.730
that brought all the politics
of the different places,

01:08:19.730 --> 01:08:21.970
but then sort of punctuates it

01:08:21.970 --> 01:08:24.463
with the significance of a certain place.

01:08:25.400 --> 01:08:27.051
I thought it was very interesting.

01:08:27.051 --> 01:08:28.680
- Thank you.

01:08:28.680 --> 01:08:31.320
- We're at 1:15 now.

01:08:31.320 --> 01:08:34.370
We have one question from Francis Martin,

01:08:34.370 --> 01:08:36.510
and if there's no objections,

01:08:36.510 --> 01:08:38.150
I'll make that the last question.

01:08:38.150 --> 01:08:39.860
But if anyone else,

01:08:39.860 --> 01:08:41.920
wants to send

01:08:41.920 --> 01:08:43.670
the question--
- I'll hang on a little bit.

01:08:43.670 --> 01:08:45.240
- you can just send it to the...

01:08:45.240 --> 01:08:46.870
Also if you have an important question

01:08:46.870 --> 01:08:47.910
they can't squeeze in,

01:08:47.910 --> 01:08:48.900
send it to the chat

01:08:48.900 --> 01:08:52.840
so Jana has at her disposal
also just for her information.

01:08:52.840 --> 01:08:54.320
Francis.

01:08:54.320 --> 01:08:55.153
- Hi, Dr. Lipmann.

01:08:55.153 --> 01:08:57.120
Thank you very much for your talk.

01:08:57.120 --> 01:08:58.060
It was wonderful.

01:08:58.060 --> 01:08:58.940
My question really,

01:08:58.940 --> 01:09:02.450
again gets to the history
writing side of things.

01:09:02.450 --> 01:09:06.100
How did language and
your limited access to,

01:09:06.100 --> 01:09:09.860
again, language, Vietnamese,
Malay, Chinese, et cetera,

01:09:09.860 --> 01:09:13.650
sort of narrow or limit the
stories you were able to tell?

01:09:13.650 --> 01:09:15.980
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:09:15.980 --> 01:09:17.710
So again, full disclosure

01:09:17.710 --> 01:09:18.543
and I say this in the book.

01:09:18.543 --> 01:09:19.970
I am an Anglophone.

01:09:19.970 --> 01:09:24.053
I am an American my languages are minimal.

01:09:25.317 --> 01:09:26.817
I actually really struggled with,

01:09:26.817 --> 01:09:28.327
"Could I do this project

01:09:28.327 --> 01:09:32.180
"as a English dominated language project?"

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:33.720
I talked to a lot of colleagues

01:09:33.720 --> 01:09:35.540
and a lot of friends

01:09:35.540 --> 01:09:36.957
and in a way,

01:09:36.957 --> 01:09:38.390
and I hate to sound,

01:09:38.390 --> 01:09:39.223
just it's hot spot,

01:09:39.223 --> 01:09:42.750
it's like, I had tenure.

01:09:42.750 --> 01:09:44.050
My first book was done.

01:09:44.050 --> 01:09:46.760
I did learn Spanish for the first book.

01:09:46.760 --> 01:09:47.593
I just was like,

01:09:48.740 --> 01:09:50.720
I want to follow the stories
that I found in Guam.

01:09:50.720 --> 01:09:53.680
I found that I'd done
other research in the US

01:09:53.680 --> 01:09:55.480
that wasn't as compelling to me.

01:09:55.480 --> 01:09:56.313
And I was like,

01:09:56.313 --> 01:09:58.673
these are stories that
I felt I could follow.

01:09:59.610 --> 01:10:02.980
Then I decided that I was not gonna

01:10:02.980 --> 01:10:04.950
do an oral history project attached to it.

01:10:04.950 --> 01:10:07.210
That there were other
people who knew Vietnamese

01:10:07.210 --> 01:10:09.860
and could do those oral history projects,

01:10:09.860 --> 01:10:11.340
with language 'cause that I don't have.

01:10:11.340 --> 01:10:12.862
And so I draw on.

01:10:12.862 --> 01:10:13.695
Carina's done a lot,

01:10:13.695 --> 01:10:15.170
University of California Irvine

01:10:15.170 --> 01:10:17.380
has a huge oral history collection.

01:10:17.380 --> 01:10:19.340
Other people have been doing them.

01:10:19.340 --> 01:10:22.300
I largely decided that I was not going try

01:10:22.300 --> 01:10:24.780
to conduct a significant
oral history project.

01:10:24.780 --> 01:10:26.120
I did some interviews.

01:10:26.120 --> 01:10:27.730
I did some interviews in the project,

01:10:27.730 --> 01:10:32.073
but I was gonna be cognizant
of my language limitations.

01:10:33.100 --> 01:10:34.620
I also, in some ways

01:10:34.620 --> 01:10:36.680
going to Erik's question
in terms of sites,

01:10:36.680 --> 01:10:38.650
it should not be too hard to figure out

01:10:38.650 --> 01:10:41.390
that I picked sites that were
British or American colonies.

01:10:41.390 --> 01:10:43.870
That means you have a lot of
English language documents.

01:10:43.870 --> 01:10:48.130
Between Guam, Malaysia, Hong
Kong, and the Philippines,

01:10:48.130 --> 01:10:49.990
I would say the place
where the language was,

01:10:49.990 --> 01:10:52.170
the biggest obstacle would be Malaysia.

01:10:52.170 --> 01:10:55.320
More of the documents there
are in Malay and Chinese.

01:10:55.320 --> 01:10:56.490
In a way that Hong Kong

01:10:56.490 --> 01:10:57.770
clearly, if I knew Chinese,

01:10:57.770 --> 01:11:00.500
I'd have access to a
whole another archive,

01:11:00.500 --> 01:11:02.830
but there's plenty in English.

01:11:02.830 --> 01:11:04.943
Similarly with the Philippines.

01:11:05.820 --> 01:11:08.550
Most of the formal
documents in the Philippines

01:11:08.550 --> 01:11:09.600
are in English.

01:11:09.600 --> 01:11:12.720
Even though you have to go log newspapers,

01:11:12.720 --> 01:11:15.870
it would have been useful
if I spoke Visayan,

01:11:15.870 --> 01:11:16.883
at some point,

01:11:17.810 --> 01:11:19.380
that it wasn't necessary

01:11:19.380 --> 01:11:21.490
from an archival standpoint.

01:11:21.490 --> 01:11:22.980
I also picked the sites

01:11:22.980 --> 01:11:26.220
where I knew there would
be English sources.

01:11:26.220 --> 01:11:28.620
But I think there's
plenty of work to be done

01:11:28.620 --> 01:11:31.873
for people with language
skills better than mine.

01:11:33.083 --> 01:11:35.400
I just decided that it was
the book I wanted to write

01:11:35.400 --> 01:11:39.370
and that I was able to
find enough English sources

01:11:39.370 --> 01:11:41.870
and then hire some translators
when I needed them.

01:11:43.250 --> 01:11:44.443
So I hope that helps.

01:11:47.505 --> 01:11:49.520
(keyboard clicking)

01:11:49.520 --> 01:11:50.353
- Great.

01:11:50.353 --> 01:11:51.750
Thanks Jana.

01:11:51.750 --> 01:11:53.950
I think I'm gonna bring
this formally to close

01:11:53.950 --> 01:11:56.030
and stop the recording.

01:11:56.030 --> 01:11:57.910
But I want everyone (clapping) to join me

01:11:57.910 --> 01:12:00.723
in thanking Jana for a wonderful talk.

